Here I'll do a smoke test of the whole app in Node using stubs to detect runtime errors (undefined variables, etc.). I can stub THREE, document, and window, then load the scripts in order and call the boot flow. Three.js's geometry classes will need to be stubbed... main.js constructs THREE.PlaneGeometry and such with real math (accesses attributes). Stubbing PlaneGeometry is complicated, because main reads `geo.attributes.position` with count/getX etc.

Alternative: use the real three.js module in node? Does three.min.js r128's UMD work in node? It expects a browser environment (document?) — in a module context THREE = ... it might load without DOM. r128's UMD checks for `typeof self !== 'undefined'`... might work: `const THREE = require('./lib/three.min.js')` — UMD sets module.exports. Then I can stub document/window minimally and run the init functions (everything except buildTerrainMesh. That one uses PlaneGeometry, which works headless too — BufferGeometry doesn't need DOM). CanvasTexture needs document — the ROME.makeLabel used in builders creates a canvas via document.createElement → needs a stub with getContext returning a stub. I can stub document.createElement to return a fake canvas with getContext returning an object where no-op functions and createLinearGradient... makeLabel uses ctx.fillRect, font assignment, fillText — no problem with a no-op Proxy.

THREE.CanvasTexture in node: does the constructor just store the image? No problem. THREE.Mesh, Scene, etc. are all headless-safe.

renderer: skip initThree and directly call ROME.initMaterials() and each init* with a scene. Also test that ROME.samplePath and makeStrip work (InstancedMesh works in node).

Let me write test.js in a temp directory:

```js
global.window = global;
const canvasStub = () => ({ getContext: () => new Proxy({}, { get: (t,p)=> (p==='canvas'? null : ()=>{}) , set: ()=>true }) , width:0, height:0 });
global.document = { createElement: () => new Proxy({ style:{} }, { get:(t,p)=> p in t ? t[p] : ()=>{ throw new Error('missing') } }) };
```
Hmm, a Proxy that returns a function for every get breaks property setting (font). Use a plain object:
```js
function fakeCtx(){ return { fillRect(){}, fillText(){}, }; }
global.document = { createElement: () => ({ getContext: fakeCtx, style:{} }) };
```
THREE.CanvasTexture(canvas) — r128's CanvasTexture just sets this.image = canvas. OK.

Then:
```js
const THREE = require('./lib/three.min.js');
global.THREE = THREE;
// load scripts in order
const fs = require('fs');
const files = ['terrain','temples','colosseum','circuses','bathes','walls','buildings','obelisks','columns','triumphArches','aqueducts','fountains','villas','roads'];
