ROME.initMaterials();
const scene = new THREE.Scene();
for each init function... call it
```
The scripts use `var walkSpeed` etc. — only main.js. eval in function scope is fine.

After that, also call buildTerrainMesh? It's in main.js which references `scene`/`renderer` globals... main.js defines `let scene, camera...` and functions that use them. In node, require main.js (with require, window/document must exist, and the top level of main.js is just declarations + a window.addEventListener call — window must have addEventListener: global.window = global with a no-op addEventListener). Then set the scene and call buildTerrainMesh? The `scene` variable is module-level `let` inside main.js, so unless we evaluate the whole boot, we can't easily set it from the test... Simpler: eval main.js too, and manually invoke the internal functions? They're in the eval scope; if I eval everything together in one context I can access the function declarations.

Plan: in one node process, eval all the js files (except ui which uses a DOM element) in order: terrain...roads, main (main is safe at top level: `let scene...`, function definitions, `window.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', ...)` — provide global.addEventListener no-op so it doesn't auto-boot; or make it actually boot? It calls initThree → WebGLRenderer requires canvas → skip).

After that, set scene and call buildTerrainMesh? buildTerrainMesh uses `scene` — same scope, so if I eval main.js in the same scope, I can access `buildTerrainMesh`... eval executes in the current scope: top-level `let scene` creates a scope-local binding — accessible from subsequent code in the same eval? Each file is a separate eval string; `let scene` in file 14 creates a binding in the surrounding (function/global) scope — in non-strict eval, top-level let goes into the global lexical environment, accessible from later evals. But I can't write `scene = ...` — actually I can: the assignment resolves to the global lexical let.

